  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 27 September 2021, the Parliament of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina adopted the new FBiH Employment Strategy (2021-2027), and its action plan. 

The employment strategy proposes both demand and supply side measures. It contains measures to tackle the specific labour market disadvantage of women, youth, and other categories at risk of labour market exclusion. Starting in the second half of 2020, the ILO facilitated the activities of a tripartite multi-ministerial working group for the elaboration of the employment strategy of the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina. The ILO&#x2019;s technical assistance focused on supporting an accurate and gender- transformative diagnostic, as well as a policy review.  Capacity development interventions facilitated the formulation of policy outcomes and targets that take into consideration the disproportionate labour market disadvantage of women. The ILO also facilitated a dialogue on specific measures targeting young people. 

In Bosnia and Herzegovina, the work of state and entity-level institutions in charge of preparing the ground for the youth guarantee relied significantly on the ILO&#x2019;s facilitation of peer-learning and triangular cooperation with other public employment services, via South-South and Triangular Cooperation.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.1.1. Number of member States with new generation of national employment policies addressing country-specific future of work challenges</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Business Organisation for the IT sector (BIT Alliance) validated and adopted a strategy document that prioritizes interventions into skills development to reduce skills mismatches. The strategy includes action points on gender equality. The ILO developed a diagnostic background report, that identified skills mismatches and anticipated future skills needs in the IT sector.

A strategy workshop based on the ILO Rapid STED methodology helped stakeholders&#x2019; prioritize and agree on intervention into skills development based on the prior diagnostic.

The Rapid STED  process implementation with the ILO technical support and guidance also helped to develop institutional capacity of key stakeholders.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">5.1.1. Number of member States that have applied ILO approaches to measure skills mismatches and/or anticipate future skill needs at national and/or sectoral level</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
